  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1247 AM MDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nFor Monday, latest models in good agreement of the upper high and \nmid level thermal trough shifting slowly south and east through the \nday. This will likely keep hot temperatures in place across the \nsoutheast plains. However, surface reflection of thermal trough \nsends a northerly surge across northern portions of the plains \nthrough the morning, and developing surface upslope across the \nmtn/plains interface will lead to better chances of clouds and \nafternoon storm development, with the continued influx of \navailable moisture within the weak westerly flow aloft. So for \nnow, will let the next shift get a better handle on temperatures \nand the need for any Heat Advisories for Monday. Otherwise, with \nthe continued influx of available moisture, will once again see a \ngood coverage of afternoon showers and storms over and near the \nhigher terrain, with the continued threat of heavy rainfall and \nthe potential for flash flooding, especially for the new burn \nareas. \n\nThrough the middle and end of the work week, the upper high remains \nprogged to build south and west across the Southern Rockies and \nlooks to centered across the Four Corners region by the end of the \nweek. This will lead to a slow cooling trend, with temperatures at \nto slightly above seasonal levels through the end of the week. \nThe continued wetter trend remains areawide, as monsoonal moisture\ncontinues to increase within developing west to northwest flow \nacross the region. The best coverage of daily showers and storms \nwill remain over and near the higher terrain through period, \nthough, the southeast plains will see increasing chances of \nshowers and storms through the middle and end of the week, with \nthe abundant moisture in place and a few embedded disturbances \nriding the periphery of the ridge. \n\nLatest longer term models and ensemble data do indicate drier air \nworking back into the Rockies into next weekend, as monsoonal moisture\nis pushed south and west of the Central Rockies. There will likely be \nenough residual moisture to support scattered afternoon showers and \nstorms over the higher terrain, though storms will likely be on the \ndrier side and less efficient rain makers.",
